# Python security

Sources: [Python security considerations](https://docs.python.org/3/library/security_warnings.html), OpenSSF Secure Coding Guide for Python, common secure-coding practice.

## Secrets & randomness

- Never hardcode API keys, tokens, or passwords — use env / secret manager
- Use `secrets` for tokens (`token_urlsafe`); never `random` for security
- Compare secrets with `secrets.compare_digest` / `hmac.compare_digest`
- Do not log secrets, full Authorization headers, or raw API keys

## Dangerous APIs (avoid with untrusted data)

- **`pickle` / `shelve` / `multiprocessing` recv**: do not unpickle untrusted input — prefer JSON
- **`subprocess`**: use argument lists with `shell=False`; never `shell=True` with user input
- **`eval` / `exec` / `logging` config with eval**: do not eval user-controlled strings
- **XML**: use defused parsers if parsing untrusted XML
- **`tempfile.mktemp`**: deprecated/racy — use safe tempfile APIs
- **`zipfile`**: guard against zip bombs / path traversal on extract
- **`http.server`**: not for production

## TLS / HTTP clients

- Prefer HTTPS; do not disable certificate verification in production
- Use default SSL contexts (`ssl.create_default_context`) unless you have a documented reason

## Paths & isolation

- Prefer running without unsafe `sys.path` prepend (`-P` / `PYTHONSAFEPATH` when applicable)
- Do not serve application source from a public web root

## Crypto

- Do not invent crypto; use vetted libraries
- Password hashing: Argon2/bcrypt/scrypt via standard libs — never plaintext or raw SHA for passwords

## Supply chain

- Pin/lock dependencies (`uv.lock`); review new deps
- Prefer scanning (e.g. Bandit, `pip-audit` / `uv` audits) in CI when adding security tooling

## This project specifically

- LLM API keys only via Settings / `.env` (gitignored)
- Do not send secrets in estimate responses or OpenAPI examples
- Bound and validate `transcription` length before calling providers (cost / abuse)
